The defendant No. 3 in its written statement heavily emphasized on the point that the instant Admiralty suit is not maintainable in its present form and manner on two courts first being the Section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861 does not attract the plaintiff&#39;s cause and secondly the plaintiff not being the consignee of the goods cannot initiate any Admiralty suit under the provision of Admiralty Court Act, 1861, the prevailing law at the date of plaintiffs alleged cause of action, which only allowed to initiate Admiralty Suit where the claimant has suffered any damage in respect of the imported goods. 

 12. While arguing on this Court the learned Advocate Mr Md Raisuddin Ahmed, appearing on behalf of the defendant No. 3 taken this Court through the provision of Section of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861 (24 VICT C10) and strenuously argued that this suit has been instituted under the provision of section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861, as the said law was in effect at the time of institution of the suit in the year 1999. But such provision of section 6 of the said Act does not give any jurisdiction to this Court to adjudicate the instant suit since the plaintiff does not allege any damage done to the goods or to any part thereof by the negligence or misconduct of or for any breach of duty or breach of contract on the part of the owner, master or crew members of the vessel.&nbsp; 

 13. The learned Advocate Mr Raisuddin Ahmed further argued that the plaintiff not being the consignee is not entitled to maintain Admiralty suit as he is not in the privity of contract with the vessel owner. In support of his arguments the learned Advocate Mr Raisuddin Ahmed relied on the case of Al-Sayer Navigation reported in 12 BLD (AD) 69, the case of Modina Vegetable Ltd reported in 45 DLR 741 and also on the case of Eastern Steamship Ltd reported in 1983 BLD 178.&nbsp; 

 14. On the other hand the learned Advocate Ms Karishma Jahan, appearing on behalf of the plaintiff, developed her arguments on manifold counts in favour of maintainability of the instant Admiralty suit. Firstly she submits that she also relies upon the referred cases of Al-Sayer Navigation, Modify? Vegetable, Eastern Steamship Ltd. On the point of coming to the conclusion that Section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861 has empowered this court to entertain as Admiralty Suit where there occurred a breach of contract as occurred in the instant case. In expounding the argument the learned Advocate Ms Karishma Jahan categorically taken this court through the terms of contract as expressed in the Bills of Lading and argued that the Bills of Lading is the contract of carriage and the carrier is duty bound to comply with the stipulation as made therein and to deliver the loaded cargo in time, being one of the vital stipulation of the Bills of Lading any violation of the same attracts the provision of Section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861.&nbsp; 

