 21. In the case of Eastern Steamship Enterprise Pvt Ltd vs Bangladesh Agricultural Research Institute reported- 1983 BLD 178 his lordships Mr Justice MH Rahman, as his lordship was then, held in this Admiralty Bench, while considering the provision of Section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861 as under:&nbsp; 

 22. In the aforesaid decisions, their Lordships have confirmed two points for maintainability of the Admiralty Suit under section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861; i.e.; Admiralty Suit against the carrier is maintainable where damage (i) done to the goods or any part thereof or (ii) where there was breach of any duty or breach of contract on the part of the owner, master or crews of the ship.&nbsp; 

 25. In the case before us the plaintiff is the importer of the consignment and accordingly he has been mentioned in the Bills of Lading as &quot;notifying party.&quot; Annexure &quot;B&quot; evidenced that there exists an agreement, dated 29-6-1999 in between the defendant No.6 Glencore Grain Ltd, the supplier of the cargo and the plaintiff for import of Crude Degummed Soybean Oil (CDSO) while exhibit &#39;C&#39; is the L/C opened by the plaintiff through the Standard Chartered Bank, Dhaka in favour of beneficiary Glencore Grain Limited. Exhibit &#39;E&#39; reveals that the master of the defendant vessel &#39;MT Komandarm Fedko&#39; issued the Bills of Lading being Nos. 01A, 01B, 01C, 01D, 01E, 01F, at the port of loading San Lorenzo, Argentina, wherein the plaintiff&#39;s name has been incorporated as notifying party. All these exhibits have been submitted in photocopy and the learned Advocate Ms Karishma Jahan informed this Court that as admittedly the plaintiff has taken delivery of the cargo, albeit late; the original copy of the shipping documents have been submitted and now lying under the Customs authority. Therefore, it appears that the plaintiff as the importer of the said consignment entered into the shoe of the shipper who lawfully transferred the title to the Cargo to the plaintiff by endorsing and handing over the shipping documents through banking channel. Thereafter, upon retirement of the shipping documents from the L/C opening Bank the plaintiff obtained every authority to challenge, in an Admiralty Suit, against any damage done to the cargo or to its any part or against any breach of contract of carriage. All the legal entitlement of the shipper now shifted to the importer and the importer became entitled to enforce the legal right which the shipper was entitled under the bills of lading. This our proposition has the support from the great Admiralty author CARVER in his treatise &quot;Bills of Lading&quot;. Therefore the question raised before this Court as to the alleged delay in delivery of the cargo is well within the terms of breach of contract i.e. the terms and conditions of the Bill of Lading which squarely adjudicatable by the Admiralty Court as comes under the provisions of Section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861. Accordingly this suit appears to be happily maintainable in this jurisdiction. Therefore the issue No. 1 is disposed off in favour of the plaintiff.&nbsp; 

 31. The provision of section 6 of the Admiralty Court Act, 1861 covers the cause of action arising out of breach of contract in maintaining an Admiralty suit in this jurisdiction. This court has already found that the defendant vessel reached Chittagong Port very late in derogation of the terms and conditions of the contract of Carriage and as such this court finds that the plaintiff has good cause of action for the instant Admiralty suit under the bundle of facts and circumstances as stated in the plaint. Therefore, the issue No. 2 is disposed of in favour of the plaintiff and against the defendant No. 2.&nbsp; 

