 60. The learned Advocate Mr. Ramjan Ali Sikder while appreciated his own difficulties in substantiating his prayer for taking judicial notice of the documents, further relied on the alternative relief, as he has prayed in the application, to the extent that the PW1 be allowed to be re-called and to prove those documents as filed along with the application and argued that ends of justice requires that this court allows the prayer since this court is satisfied that the plaintiff has cause of action against the defendants. In this respect the learned Advocate Mr. Ramjan Ali Sikder relied on two decisions reported in 48 DLR 593 and 22 BLD 217 and further argued that the court can allow a recall of the witness at any stage of the suit, even at the stage of passing of judgment.&nbsp; 

 61. Upon perusal of the said decisions it appears that in the case of Morsalin vs Nurul Huda reported in 48 DLR 593 his lordships Mr. Syed Amirul Islam while sitting singly in the revisional jurisdiction considered the point and held as under:&nbsp; 

 63. It appears that admittedly the plaintiff has failed to submit those documents with the plaint and under the provision of order 7 rule 18 of the Code of Civil procedure, 1908 the plaintiff is barred to file those documents unless the court directs otherwise. In the case of Kazi Nuruzzaman reported in 58 DLR 307 his lordships Mr. Siddiqur Rahman J in a Division Bench while considering the provision of Order 8, rule 1(5) of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 as to the filing of documents with the Written Statement held that the court will be in a position to grant leave to file documents in unavoidable and exceptional circumstances. Therefore, this court has to decide, whether the documents as filed by the plaintiff, at this stage of delivery of judgment can be allowed to be considered either through judicial notice or through recall of the PW I for proving those documents and to mark the same as exhibits.&nbsp; 

 66. So far the learned Advocate Mr. Ramjan Ali Sikder has relied upon the principle, as enunciated in 48 DLR 593 and 22 BLD 217, it appears that these cases are regarding the recalling of the witnesses for adducing oral evidence before the court at the stage of deli-very of judgment and not regarding filing any document at the stage of delivery of judgment, which has not been filed either with the plaint or at the time of examination of the PW I. Therefore, the application as filed by the plain-tiff has no merit to be considered at this stage.&nbsp;
